<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Young Ottomans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Yeni Osmanlılar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> established in 1865 by Ottoman intellectuals who believed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms did not go far enough. They sought to preserve and modernize the Empire through constitutional government on the European model, kept rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>; among them were the writers İbrahim Şinasi, Namık Kemal, Ali Suavi and Ziya Pasha. In 1876 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha">Midhat Pasha</a> conspired with them to overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdul Aziz, and Abdul Hamid II reluctantly promulgated the Ottoman constitution of 1876, opening the First Constitutional Era. Abdul Hamid suspended it in 1878, but the Young Turks later repeated their efforts in the Young Turk Revolution of 1908.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/young-ottomans.jpg" alt="Young Ottomans" width="330" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdullah frères · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Namık_Kemal_1878-1.1R_V01-1.2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
